os9dsk on OS-9 -- what was changed=================================Bob Devries' os9dsk, 1997, "may be freely distributed".  ORIG/ holds therelease as it came out of the LHa archive, unpacked by this disk's own`lha': the source, both makefiles, and the documentation.ONE FILE WAS REPLACED: coco_direct.h.The header the release shipped said in its own first line that itwas "modified from the OSK version", so it derived from Microware's<direct.h>, and this collection does not carry Microware's headers.What the program needs from it is a description of what is ON THEMEDIA -- a disk format rather than a system interface -- so thestructures are declared here instead, with the member names os9dsk'sown code uses.  A CoCo directory entry gives the name 29 charactersand the descriptor address 3 bytes, where OS-9/68000 uses 28 and along; that difference is the whole reason this program exists.The rebuilt module is the same size to the byte as the one builtagainst the original header, and reads the sample disk identically.ORIG/ therefore holds the release MINUS that one header.OTHERWISE NOTHING WAS CHANGED.  It was written for OS-9 and ships a makefile.68000;what is here is a rebuild from that source with the collection's driver, soit is trap-free like everything else rather than being the author's ownbinary.  The recipe follows the makefile: eight sources, linking alib.WHAT IT READS.  A .DSK file is a sector-by-sector image of a Color Computerfloppy, the format Jeff Vavasour's CoCo emulator uses.  os9dsk reads theOS-9 ones; `rsdsk', beside it, reads the RS-DOS (Disk Extended BASIC) ones.Given the wrong kind it says so plainly -- "Not an OS-9 .DSK file!" -- whichis how you tell which sort you have.The sample image in DOC/os9dsk is a 1985 disk and lists as one: CMDS,BASIC09, LIB, DEFS and C, with dates in November of that year.